* Bracket contents in the format lattice (Turchin 1980 2.3).
*
* A bracket is a term whose *contents* are themselves an expression, so a
* format that stops at "it is a bracket" is leaving information on the table.
* With the contents described, `refal check --strict` can refute this call
* without knowing anything about the run:
*
*   OnlyNumber accepts  [([N])]   -- a bracket whose contents are a number
*   this call passes    [([C])]   -- a bracket whose contents are a character
*
* The two sets cannot overlap, so no argument this program can supply will ever
* be accepted. `--classic` still accepts the program, because nothing about the
* language changed -- only the diagnosis did. That separation is the whole point
* of the mode switch: `--classic` is a pure Refal-5 conformance mode.
*
* A character, a number and an identifier are three different symbols, which is
* what makes `[C]` and `[N]` disjoint. An `s.`-variable widens to `[S]` and is
* never refuted by a literal kind -- the safe direction.

$EXTERN Prout;

$ENTRY Go {
  = <Prout <OnlyNumber ('a')>>;
}

OnlyNumber {
  (1) = 'number';
}
